<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kutadgu Bilig</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1069</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kutadgu Bilig ('Wisdom of Royal Glory') is a long didactic poem in Karakhanid Turkic written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Balasaguni</a> and presented to the khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> around 1069. Framed as dialogues between a king, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and a sage, it is a mirror for princes on justice, statecraft and the good life, and one of the earliest major works of Turkic Islamic literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig.jpg" alt="Kutadgu Bilig" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QutadughuBiliq_wien_p.10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
